+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a service for larder to run, or weighs one that no decision record settles. It holds what the
+team favors. Written in October 2026 after a proposal to add Elasticsearch for pantry item search
+([issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md)) argued the same trade-off as Redis for sessions in 2023 and Redis for Celery in
+March 2025, each time from the start.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### A new service must do what Postgres cannot, and two volunteers must be able to restore it
+
+Larder runs one Postgres database and one web app, with its worker. Each service added beside them is one more thing
+a volunteer must keep alive and restore on a Saturday night, and when only one volunteer can, larder stops when that
+volunteer is away. In March 2025 the Redis instance holding Celery's queue evicted 300 receipt jobs, and the one
+volunteer who knew it spent a day restoring it and resending receipts by hand.
+
+Rules out a service that duplicates what Postgres already does, such as a cache, a queue, a session store or a search
+engine, and a service only one volunteer can restore. It does not rule out a service larder does not run: the card
+gateway is outside, and a hosted service nobody has to operate is welcome.
+
+A proposal starts by showing what Postgres does on the problem. In October 2026 Postgres full-text search on the item
+name brought a search for "rice" from four seconds to 40 ms and found "Rice, brown", where Elasticsearch was proposed.
+
+Applied in [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
